course: dictionary
lesson: 1125
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. «Breakout» can mean an escape, a sudden outbreak, a very successful newcomer, or a small group in a meeting. To find the right meaning, read the words around it. They are its context, and they leave clues.
explain: «Spots come mainly from hormones, skin and genes; one chocolate bar will not cause a breakout, though very sugary diets may matter for some people.» The clues are spots, skin and cause, so a breakout here means a sudden start of spots on the skin. Try your meaning in the sentence: "one chocolate bar will not cause a sudden start of spots" still makes sense.
explain: Now look at clues in other sentences. A prison, guards and bars point to an escape. A famous singer on the news points to a breakout star who has suddenly become successful. A teacher sending pupils away from the main class points to a breakout room. Always ask: what is happening here?
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Breakout» (noun) means an escape from a prison or other place by force.
fact: «Breakout» (noun) also means a sudden start or spread of something, like spots on the skin.
fact: «Breakout» (adjective) means suddenly very successful, as in a breakout hit.
fact: A breakout room is a small group split off from a bigger meeting or class.
exercise: «One chocolate bar will not cause a breakout.» In this sentence, what does breakout mean? | A very successful hit | A small group split off from a class | An escape from a prison | *A sudden start of spots on the skin
exercise: Teacher says, «Pupils, you will go to breakout rooms now.» What is a breakout room? | A prison cell | A room with a big window | *A small group split off from a bigger meeting or class | A room for cooking
exercise: The news says, «She is a breakout star.» What does breakout suggest? | *She has suddenly become very successful | She is sitting in a small group | She escaped from a prison | She has spots on her face
test: «The prisoners' breakout made the news.» What does breakout mean here? | A very successful new hit | *An escape from a prison by force | A sudden start of spots | A small group split off from a class
test: «The breakout film was a surprise hit.» What does breakout mean in this sentence? | *Suddenly very successful | A small group in a meeting | An escape by force | Spots on the skin
exam: «One chocolate bar will not cause a breakout, though very sugary diets may matter.» Which meaning of breakout fits best here? | A breakout star who is famous | *A sudden start of spots on the skin | An escape from a prison | A small group split off from a class
